@packages/cli import { SigilCli }
@integrations/skills/sigil/design-conversation.sigil import { SigilDesignConversation }
@integrations/skills/sigil/review-gate.sigil import { SigilReviewGate }

component SigilDesignCompilationReview {
  goal {
    Consume compiler-owned semantic-readiness and architecture evaluation as
    provisional evidence for exact written and unwritten Sigil candidates without
    transferring design decisions or mutation authority to the compiler.
  }

  interface {
DesignCompilationReview {
      evaluates each exact unwritten Sigil candidate through SigilCli compilation
      focus design before ReviewGate for sigil-change

      consumes deterministic-foundation, semantic-readiness, and architecture-design
      results from the exact proposal generation

      permits proposal review when the complete design compilation is green or when
      every yellow finding is explicitly reviewed and accepted as nonblocking by the
      human user
    }
  }
}

expand SigilDesignCompilationReview {
  state {
DesignCompilationReview {
      Design compilation is unavailable, incomplete, red, yellow, or green for one
      exact proposal generation and reviewed scope.
    }
  }

  logic {
DesignCompilationReview {
      After design intent and applicable external guidance are sufficiently resolved,
      open one proposal compilation session for the selected scope.

      Submit each complete candidate source set as a proposal generation without
      writing it to the selected workspace. The compiler session is an owner- private
      proposal workspace for evidence only, not the mutation target.

      Use red and yellow diagnostics to investigate evidence and return confirmed
      material problems to DesignConversation. Revise red candidates and yellow
      candidates with unresolved findings.

      After concept grouping or any semantic change, evaluate a new complete
      generation.

      Submit only an exact green proposal generation, or a yellow generation whose
      every finding the human explicitly accepted as nonblocking, together with its
      report fingerprint and finding dispositions to ReviewGate for sigil-change.

      After ReviewGate approves the exact target-workspace scope and complete source
      change set, materialize only that approved proposal in the target workspace.
      Materialization is forbidden while ReviewGate is blocked or review-required.
      Then run deterministic validation and compilation focus design against the
      written source before glossary extraction or implementation review.
    }
  }

  constraints {
DesignCompilationReview {
      Compiler green and reviewed yellow are provisional design evidence and do not
      approve Sigil mutation, glossary mutation, or implementation.

      The skill does not duplicate semantic-readiness or architecture-design judgment
      already owned by the compiler focus closure.

      External-guidance applicability, design decisions, correction conversations,
      concept-grouping approval, glossary extraction, and ReviewGate remain owned by
      the host workflow.

      An unavailable, incomplete, or red required design compilation prevents
      ReviewGate for sigil-change from returning ready. Yellow also prevents ready
      until the human explicitly reviews every finding and accepts it as nonblocking.

      ReviewGate evidence identifies the target workspace root, target paths,
      complete resulting source, exact session, generation, base fingerprint,
      proposal fingerprint, target, profile, focus, and completed stages. Session
      evidence never grants permission to mutate the target workspace.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    CompilerDrivenDesignReview {
      Decision: Use SigilCompiler focus design as the semantic-readiness and
      modularity evaluator for exact written and unwritten Sigil candidates.

      Scope: Governs SigilSkill design review before sigil-change approval and after
      approved Sigil is written; excludes human design decisions, external-guidance
      research, glossary extraction, and mutation authority.

      Trade-offs: Semantic proposal work now requires an available configured
      compiler evaluator and may require repeated agentic compilation.

      Design issues addressed: Separate host and compiler review checklists can
      produce different readiness conclusions and cannot evaluate unwritten proposals
      through ordinary Sigil tools.

      Discarded alternatives: Retaining duplicate host semantic and modularity review
      was rejected because the compiler design closure already owns those
      evaluations.

      Consequences: One compiler report evaluates deterministic validity, semantic
      readiness, and architecture while ReviewGate remains the sole mutation
      authority.
    }

    ReviewedYellowDesignEvidence {
      Decision: Permit a complete yellow design compilation to proceed only after the
      human explicitly reviews and accepts every finding as nonblocking.

      Scope: Governs provisional design evidence for Sigil proposal, glossary, and
      implementation workflows; excludes unavailable, incomplete, and red reports.

      Trade-offs: Noncritical warnings may remain intentionally deferred, and their
      dispositions must travel with the exact report evidence.

      Design issues addressed: Treating every warning as blocking prevents informed
      human judgment, while treating yellow as automatic readiness hides unresolved
      findings.

      Discarded alternatives: Automatic rejection and automatic acceptance of every
      yellow report were rejected.

      Consequences: Reviewed warnings remain visible and scoped to one report; new or
      changed findings require another disposition.
    }
  }

  cases {
DesignCompilationReview {
      A red proposal generation with contradictory state ownership returns to
      DesignConversation before concept grouping or glossary extraction.

      A yellow proposal with one unresolved warning remains blocked. When the human
      explicitly accepts every finding as nonblocking, its report and dispositions
      may proceed as provisional evidence.

      An ungrouped green proposal proceeds to concept grouping, then compiles a new
      complete generation before glossary extraction.

      An unavailable compilation reports the unavailable compiler evidence, keeps the
      exact candidate unchanged, and may retry only after capability is restored.

      An incomplete compilation reports completed and missing stages plus available
      budget or evidence limitations, keeps ReviewGate from ready, and permits a new
      complete generation after the limitation is resolved.
    }
  }
}
